<p>There is no single Houston property tax rate. Your rate is the sum of every taxing unit that covers your address: the City of Houston, Harris County, your school district, the flood control district, the Port of Houston, Harris Health, the community college district and, in many areas, a MUD. For a typical home inside the city and Houston ISD, the main 2025 adopted rates add up to about $2.13 per $100 of value before exemptions.</p>
<p>Below is how that total is built, each unit's 2025 rate from official notices and county reporting, what changed for 2026, a worked example with homestead exemptions, and the practical ways to pay less.</p>

<h2>How the Houston Property Tax Rate Is Built</h2>
<p>Texas has no state property tax. Instead, local governments each set their own rate every year, and the Harris County Tax Office collects them on one combined bill. The Harris Central Appraisal District (HCAD) sets your property's value; it does not set rates.</p>
<p>Each rate is stated in dollars per $100 of taxable value. The basic formula is simple:</p>
<blockquote>Tax owed = taxable value x rate / 100, calculated separately for each taxing unit, then added together.</blockquote>
<p>"Taxable value" is different for each unit because exemptions differ. A school district must give a $140,000 homestead exemption, while the city and county use their own optional exemptions. That is why you cannot just add up the rates and multiply by your home's market value.</p>

<h2>2025 Adopted Rates for a Typical Houston Home</h2>
<p>The table below covers a home inside the City of Houston and Houston ISD, in Harris County, with no MUD or other special district. These are tax year 2025 rates (the bills mailed in fall 2025 and due January 31, 2026).</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Taxing unit</th><th>2025 rate per $100</th><th>Source</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Houston ISD</td><td>$0.8783</td><td>District rate adopted October 2025, as reported by the Houston Chronicle</td></tr>
<tr><td>City of Houston</td><td>$0.519190</td><td>City of Houston tax rate notice; adopted October 2025</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harris County</td><td>$0.38096</td><td>Commissioners Court, as reported by local media</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harris Health (hospital district)</td><td>$0.18761</td><td>Harris County Tax Office notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Houston Community College</td><td>$0.098802</td><td>HCC notice of adopted tax rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harris County Flood Control District</td><td>$0.04966</td><td>Harris County Tax Office notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Port of Houston Authority</td><td>$0.00590</td><td>Harris County Tax Office notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harris County Department of Education</td><td>$0.004798</td><td>HCDE board announcement</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total before exemptions</strong></td><td><strong>about $2.125</strong></td><td>Sum of the above</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A few notes on the list:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Houston ISD</strong> is the largest piece. Its 2025 rate rose from $0.8489 to $0.8783 after the board cited Hurricane Beryl costs. Homes in Spring Branch ISD, Cy-Fair ISD, Katy ISD or Alief ISD use those districts' rates instead.</li>
<li><strong>Houston Community College</strong> now brands itself Houston City College, but the taxing district is the same. Its 2025 rate was about 2.7% higher than 2024.</li>
<li><strong>The county family of units</strong> (Harris County, Flood Control, Harris Health and the Port) is set by Commissioners Court. Together they came to roughly $0.624 per $100 for 2025.</li>
<li><strong>Not every Houston address</strong> pays all of these. Parts of the city sit in Fort Bend or Montgomery County, and some neighborhoods carry extra special-district taxes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>MUDs, PIDs and Other Special Districts</h2>
<p>Many homes in unincorporated Harris County, and some inside the city limits, also pay a municipal utility district (MUD) tax. MUDs issue bonds to build water, sewer and drainage systems in new developments, then levy a tax to repay them. MUD rates vary widely from district to district, and in some newer subdivisions the MUD rate alone can be a meaningful share of the total bill.</p>
<p>You may also see a public improvement district (PID), management district, emergency services district (ESD) outside the city, or a tax increment reinvestment zone listed on your account. If you are buying, ask for the full list of taxing units before you write an offer. Our guide to <a href="/blog/mud-tax-texas/">MUD taxes in Texas</a> explains how they work.</p>

<h2>What Changed for Tax Year 2026</h2>
<p>Taxing units set 2026 rates between late summer and fall 2026, and those rates will appear on bills mailed this fall and due January 31, 2027. Here is what had been reported as of this writing:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Harris County units:</strong> In September 2026, Commissioners Court passed 2026 rates of $0.41750 for Harris County, $0.05266 for Flood Control, $0.19528 for Harris Health and $0.00603 for the Port, according to FOX 26 Houston. The county rate is about 9.6% higher than 2025.</li>
<li><strong>City of Houston:</strong> Council was reported in October 2026 to have kept the rate unchanged at $0.519190.</li>
<li><strong>Houston ISD:</strong> In June 2026 the board approved a proposed total rate of $0.8421, which Community Impact noted was subject to change once certified values arrived. Confirm the final figure on your bill.</li>
<li><strong>HCC and HCDE:</strong> Check their notices or your tax statement for the final 2026 rates.</li>
</ul>
<p>The fastest way to confirm every final rate for your address is your account on the Harris County Tax Office site or the statewide property tax portal at <a href="https://comptroller.texas.gov/taxes/property-tax/">the Texas Comptroller's property tax page</a>.</p>

<h2>Homestead Exemptions in Harris County</h2>
<p>Exemptions reduce taxable value, not the rate. For a Houston homeowner, the main ones are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>School homestead exemption:</strong> At least $140,000 off your value for school district taxes, according to HCAD.</li>
<li><strong>Over-65 or disabled (school):</strong> An additional $60,000 for school taxes, plus a school tax ceiling that caps that portion of your bill.</li>
<li><strong>Harris County optional exemption:</strong> HCAD states that Harris County provides a 20% optional homestead exemption to all homeowners.</li>
<li><strong>City of Houston:</strong> City budget materials describe a 20% residential homestead exemption, plus a separate, much larger exemption for residents 65 or older or disabled (raised to $260,000 in 2022).</li>
<li><strong>10% homestead cap:</strong> The taxable appraised value of a homestead generally cannot rise more than 10% a year, even if market value jumps more.</li>
</ul>
<p>Other units, including Flood Control, Harris Health, the Port and HCC, may offer their own exemptions. Your HCAD account lists every exemption each unit applies to your home, so check it rather than assuming. Our <a href="/blog/texas-homestead-exemption/">Texas homestead exemption guide</a> covers how and when to apply.</p>

