<h2>When Bills Arrive and the January 31 Deadline</h2>
<p>The yearly cycle runs on a predictable calendar:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>January 1:</strong> Property is valued as of this date, and homestead eligibility is generally based on it.</li>
<li><strong>Spring:</strong> HCAD mails notices of appraised value. The protest deadline is usually May 15 or 30 days after your notice is delivered, whichever is later.</li>
<li><strong>August to October:</strong> Taxing units hold hearings and adopt rates.</li>
<li><strong>October:</strong> The Texas Comptroller notes that taxing units usually mail tax bills in October.</li>
<li><strong>January 31:</strong> Payment is generally due.</li>
<li><strong>February 1:</strong> Unpaid taxes become delinquent. A 6% penalty and 1% interest apply, the penalty grows each month to 12% by July 1, and interest keeps adding 1% a month.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you escrow taxes with your mortgage, your lender pays the bill, but a big rate or value change can trigger an escrow shortage and a higher monthly payment. Homeowners 65 or older, disabled homeowners and owners of disaster-damaged homes may qualify for quarterly installment plans through the Harris County Tax Office if they act by January 31. Keep your appraisal notices, exemption approvals and tax receipts together each year.</p>
<p>[[product:accordion-file-organizer]]</p>

<h2>Ways to Lower Your Houston Property Tax Bill</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>File every exemption.</strong> General homestead, over-65, disability and disabled-veteran exemptions are the biggest and easiest savings. New buyers can apply in the year they buy and move in.</li>
<li><strong>Protest your value.</strong> Comparable sales, condition problems and repair estimates can lower your appraised value. See our step-by-step guide to <a href="/blog/protest-property-taxes-harris-county/">protesting property taxes in Harris County</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Check your account every spring.</strong> Exemptions sometimes drop off after a refinance or title change.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the rate hearings.</strong> Each unit publishes its no-new-revenue and voter-approval rates; you can comment before adoption.</li>
<li><strong>Compare total rates before you buy.</strong> Two homes at the same price can carry very different bills depending on school district and MUD.</li>
</ul>
<p>For complicated situations, such as a disputed exemption or a large commercial property, talk to a CPA, property tax consultant or attorney.</p>

<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Houston property tax rate is a stack of separate rates, and for a typical home in the city and Houston ISD the 2025 stack was about $2.13 per $100 before exemptions. Exemptions bring the effective rate well below that, while a MUD can push it higher. Look up your own taxing units, file every exemption, protest your value when the evidence supports it, and pay by January 31 to avoid penalties.</p>
